        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58817,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58818,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "<p>Tumours present in the subcutaneous or deeper soft tissues and are commonly located in the limbs. Rarely, lesions have been described elsewhere, including the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve {21356142}, the orbit {21234590}, the superior sulcus of the lung {21095359}, the mediastinum {20453425}, the retroperitoneum {20438591}, the breast {17646373}, the brain {17641273}, and the parapharyngeal space {22232718}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-11T10:53:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58819,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Venous haemangioma often presents as a longstanding slow-growing tumour. Radiological examination often shows the presence of calcification due to phleboliths.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58820,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Venous haemangioma is rare and data are limited, but these lesions occur mainly in adults.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58821,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical evolution and clinicopathological features suggest that these lesions represent vascular malformations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58822,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58823,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Venous haemangioma is ill defined and consists of dilated congested vascular spaces with areas of haemorrhage.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58824,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Venous haemangioma typically consists of large thick-walled muscular vessels, which are variably dilated and commonly display thrombosis, with occasional formation of phleboliths. Widely dilated vessels can show attenuation of their walls, mimicking a cavernous haemangioma. Elastic stains reveal the absence of an internal elastic lamina. This aids in the distinction from an arteriovenous haemangioma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58825,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58826,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58827,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> ill-defined lesion with thick-walled muscular vessels, variable dilation, and area of thrombosis; absence of internal elastic lamina.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> longstanding, slow-growing tumour, often with calcifications (phleboliths).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58828,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58829,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 272,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Deep-seated tumours are difficult to excise and can recur locally, but subcutaneous tumours do not usually recur.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]